# Exercise: Nested Conditionals
#
# Conditionals can be nested inside each other.
#
# Remember: Seq has no variables. When a comparison consumes
# a value, use `dup` beforehand so the value is still around
# for the next check.
#
# Example: Classify the top-of-stack number as positive,
# negative, or zero. The value starts on the stack.
#   dup 0 i.> if
#       drop "positive"
#   else
#       dup 0 i.< if
#           drop "negative"
#       else
#           drop "zero"
#       then
#   then
#
# Each branch drops the number (since we dup'd it) and pushes
# the resulting string.
#
# Your task: Classify the number 15.
#   - If > 10, push 3
#   - Else if > 5, push 2
#   - Else push 1
# Since 15 i.> 10, the answer should be 3.
# (Tip: `dup 10 i.>` consumes one copy for the test and
#  leaves the original for the next dup/check/drop.)
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-nested ( -- )
    15
    # Your code here - nested if/else/then
    drop 0
    # Do not edit below this line
    3 test.assert-eq
;
